{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1532907-homebrew-sanctified-plants","id":1532907,"title":"[Homebrew] Sanctified Plants","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-03-20T08:11:42-07:00","id":1532907,"title":"[Homebrew] Sanctified Plants","content":"The Rite of Spirit Awakening lets you turn plants into talens. Unfortunately, despite there being an abundance of plants they provide almost no examples of what sanctified plants might do. So here are some sanctified plants I made up.<br /> <br /> Columbine<br /> Gnosis: 6<br /> As Chiropteran Spies, but transforms into a cloud of hummingbirds. Milkweeds do the same but for butterflies.<br /> <br /> Sunflower<br /> Gnosis: 6<br /> When activated and placed on bare soil, a bundle of sanctified sunflower will draw heavy metals and other poisons from the surrounding area into itself. In particularly polluted areas the sunflowers may seem more metal than plant at the end of this process. This does not destroy the metals--if the sunflower bundle isn't disposed of properly, they can leech back into the environment.<br /> <br /> Bog Orchid<br /> Gnosis: 6<br /> The flower of a bog orchid, once sanctified, has the power of the acid bogs that birthed it. A few seconds after activation, it will start to dissolve into an acidic slurry of plant juices that easily eat through metal and flesh, though glass, quartz, and ceramics act as hard barriers. It is not, however, very quick acting. Most foes will be able to get it off them before it does more than three dice of lethal damage. If applied to an immobile foe (or one foolish enough to swallow it) it will do three dice of lethal damage per turn over a period of four turns.<br /> <br /> Sundew<br /> Gnosis: 7<br /> When swallowed, a sundew causes a werewolf's fur to animate and become sticky for the rest of the scene. In addition to a;lowing them to easily carry small objects by attacking them to their pelt, this puts opponents seeking to escape the werewolf's grip at a disadvantage (+2 difficulty to rolls).<br /> <br /> Pine<br /> Gnosis: 5<br /> When crushed, sanctified pine releases a fresh-smelling scent that neutralizes harmful vapors. Poison gases lose effectiveness in the area. Magical toxins are still effective. Hoglings loathe the scent of sanctified pine, but are not harmed by it.<br /> <br /> Anyone have others they've come up with?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-20T12:23:11-07:00","id":1532917,"title":"","content":"The Werewolf writers clearly dropped the ball when providing that Rite, but giving almost no support on what different plants can do when sanctified. It's a big failure. There should have been a good list provided in each of the Players Guides (or Storyteller Handbooks) in my opinion. The developers and writers just seem to ignore that rite. It's a shame because in my experience, this rite almost always attracts new players attention (who is then disappointed that there's no support for it).<br /> <br /> The 1e Werewolf Players Guide provides five plants and what powers they provide when sanctified. They are yaupon, plantain, catmint, basil, and peyote. Ways of the Wolf provides another eight: burdock, lodgepole pine, mullein, service berry, sweet flag, wild garlic, foxglove, and rosemary. In Valkenburg Foundation, there's a sanctified wolfsbane in one section of the book. The 1e Wendigo Tribebook mentions there are &quot;natural fetishes&quot; - things that can provide powers without Garou Rites, but do require some special handling. Purifying Plants include sage, cedar, and sweet grass, and tobacco has powers too. (I never quite liked the idea that these plants don't need the rite of sanctification, but that is something each ST can easily fix on their own.) There may be one or more other official references scattered out there, but I don't think there's many. None of these have even been collected and reprinted elsewhere to my knowledge. So that's almost twenty official powers.<br /> <br /> I have made some posts on this issue over time. Here are some links to help you.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/431972-sanctified-plants\">One Thread</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1503831-rite-of-sanctification\">Second Thread</a><br /> <br /> I also posted on plant spirits in general, since there are various special rules about them spread all over the place. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1431536-plant-spirits\">Here's the link</a>.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-21T13:15:08-07:00","id":1532954,"title":"","content":"Are you familiar with the Herbalist Primer? It was a system neutral OSR book with a huge list of plants with various &quot;magical&quot; properties typically seen in various legends as a great jumping off point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28181-a-bunch-of-gamers","name":"A Bunch of Gamers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28181_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-21T13:19:41-07:00","id":1532956,"title":"","content":"Do you think this would work the other way with banes / corrupted spirits? I see some potential with <s>Monsanto</s> Pentex GMOs, and I'm also collecting options for vampire blood gardening. Corrupted plant spirits seem like a useful element for werewolf games, especially with more subtle effects instead of putting Audrey II into a basement.<br /> <br /> Hm, thinking about it, GMO monocultures could also be an interesting vehicle for Weaver-tainted spirits. That could be a bit of a shake-up of the usual.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-21T17:41:03-07:00","id":1532973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1532907-homebrew-sanctified-plants?p=1532917#post1532917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Werewolf writers clearly dropped the ball when providing that Rite, but giving almost no support on what different plants can do when sanctified. It's a big failure. There should have been a good list provided in each of the Players Guides (or Storyteller Handbooks) in my opinion. The developers and writers just seem to ignore that rite. It's a shame because in my experience, this rite almost always attracts new players attention (who is then disappointed that there's no support for it).<br /> <br /> The 1e Werewolf Players Guide provides five plants and what powers they provide when sanctified. They are yaupon, plantain, catmint, basil, and peyote. Ways of the Wolf provides another eight: burdock, lodgepole pine, mullein, service berry, sweet flag, wild garlic, foxglove, and rosemary. In Valkenburg Foundation, there's a sanctified wolfsbane in one section of the book. The 1e Wendigo Tribebook mentions there are &quot;natural fetishes&quot; - things that can provide powers without Garou Rites, but do require some special handling. Purifying Plants include sage, cedar, and sweet grass, and tobacco has powers too. (I never quite liked the idea that these plants don't need the rite of sanctification, but that is something each ST can easily fix on their own.) There may be one or more other official references scattered out there, but I don't think there's many. None of these have even been collected and reprinted elsewhere to my knowledge. So that's almost twenty official powers.<br /> <br /> I have made some posts on this issue over time. Here are some links to help you.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/431972-sanctified-plants\">One Thread</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1503831-rite-of-sanctification\">Second Thread</a><br /> <br /> I also posted on plant spirits in general, since there are various special rules about them spread all over the place. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1431536-plant-spirits\">Here's the link</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks. I checked those out and they seem cool. Might riff off them for a few more sanctified plants.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1532907-homebrew-sanctified-plants?p=1532956#post1532956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you think this would work the other way with banes / corrupted spirits? I see some potential with <s>Monsanto</s> Pentex GMOs, and I'm also collecting options for vampire blood gardening. Corrupted plant spirits seem like a useful element for werewolf games, especially with more subtle effects instead of putting Audrey II into a basement.</div> </div> </div> </div>For corrupted plants, even before using them for talens you could get fun. Consider plants that release Wyrm toxins as allopathic chemicals, ones that have nectar or fruit that corrupts pollinators or seed dispersers, or ones that produce forever chemicals. With the right ones you can make weeding a glorious task. When awakened some of the sanctified effects could work well if you made them grosser or nastier--the bog orchid or the sundew, for instance. But you could also have them poison an area or release a cloud of Wyrm toxins that doesn't harm the Wyrm's minions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}